          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            – and specifically in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+probability+theory"} quantum probability theory
            – by a
            %em quantum operation
            or
            %em quantum channel
            one means any physically reasonable operation on, or transformation of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+states"} mixed states
            (in contrast to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            operating on
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+states"} pure states
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/information+theory"} information theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em quantum channel
          %p
            More concretely, the physical nature of quantum channels is that they unify “loss-less”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary
            transformations on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            (as known
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger evoluation
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+noise"} quantum noise
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state+collapse"} quantum state collapse
            due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
          %p
            In short, just as the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+states"} mixed states
            generalizes the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"} pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            with their objective, intrinsic and fundamental stochasticity (expressed the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Born+rule"} Born rule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+distribution"} classical stochasticity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            from pure to mixed states.
          %p
            Mathematically, with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+states"} mixed states
            represented by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrices"} density matrices
            and generally by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+linear+operators"} positive linear operators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/map"} map
            between spaces of such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superoperators"} superoperators
            (in the sense of “operators operating on operators”).
          %p
            But in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            the relevant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            are all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+space"} finite-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+matrix"} square
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi chan
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Mat
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi Mat
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  chan
                  \\;\colon\;
                  Mat_{n_1}(\mathbb{C})
                  \\longrightarrow
                  Mat_{n_2}(\mathbb{C})
                  \\,.
          %p
            Slightly more abstractly, such as in the formulation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi chan
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msubsup
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mn 1
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msubsup
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mn 2
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  chan
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathscr{H}_1 \otimes \mathscr{H}_1^\ast
                  \\longrightarrow
                  \\mathscr{H}_2 \otimes \mathscr{H}_2^\ast
          %p
            (where above
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mi dim
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_i = dim(\mathscr{H}_i)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
            of the given
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+space"} finite-dimensional Hilbert space
          %p
            The key point is that such linear maps are to qualify as quantum channels iff they suitably restrict to maps between the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrices"} density matrices
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+states"} mixed states
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msubsup
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}_i\otimes\mathscr{H}_i^\ast
          %p
            There is slight variation in the exact list of properties demanded of a quantum channel, but the key demand is that it be a “
            %em> positive map
            ” in that it takes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+operators"} positive operators
            (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+operators"} density operators
            = succeed "," do
              %em completely positive map
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensoring
            with any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+map"} identity transformation
          %p This is discussed below at:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InTermsOfPositivityConditions"} In terms of positivity conditions
          %p
            Often demanded is also that a quantum channel preserves the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            of matrices, which in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+probability"} quantum probability
            means that it preserves total probability, hence that it is the quantum analog of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+map"} stochastic map
            — through what fundamentally matters is that a quantum channel at most lowers the probability (the channel need not describe all possible outcomes, but it must not make new outcomes appear out of nowhere).
          %p
            Less often demanded (but usually the case anyway) is that a quantum channel also preserves the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+matrix"} identity matrix
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doubly+stochastic+map"} doubly stochastic map
          %p
            Beyond these abstract characterizations, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stinespring+factorization+theorem"} Stinespring factorization theorem
            characterizes quantum channels more explicitly as those maps on matrices arising as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sums"} sums
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugations"} conjugations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi chan
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ρ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi w
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msubsup
                    %mi E
                    %mi w
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  chan
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\rho
                  \\;\mapsto\;
                  \\underset{w}{\sum}
                  E_w \cdot \rho \cdot E_w^\dagger
          %p
            by certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuples"} tuples
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_w)_{w \colon W}
            of linear operators (“
            %em> Kraus operators
            ”). Much of the discussion of quantum channels in the literature proceeds by manipulating such
            %em Kraus decompositions
            of quantum channels.
          %p This is discussed below at:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InTermsOfKrausDecompositions"} In terms of Kraus decompositions
          %p
            For example, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+quantum+channel"} unitary quantum channel
            describing a loss-less
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gate"} quantum gate
            is given by a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary
            Kraus operator as
          #eq:UnitaryChannelInIdeaSection.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi chan
                    %mi U
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ρ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mo †
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  chan_U
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\rho
                  \\;\mapsto\;
                  U \cdot \rho \cdot U^\dagger
                  \\,,
          %p
            which on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+states"} pure states
            among
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+states"} mixed states
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo ≔
                    %mrow
                      %mo |
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ⟩
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⟨
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_{|\psi\rangle} \coloneqq \left\vert \psi \right\rangle \left\langle \psi \right\vert
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gate"} quantum gate
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi chan
                    %mi U
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  chan_U
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\rho_{\vert\psi \rangle}
                  \\;\mapsto\;
                  \\rho_{ U \vert \psi \rangle }
                  \\,.
          %p
            On the other extreme, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            in a measurement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis+of+a+vector+space"} basis
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi W
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi.mathscript ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{W}{\oplus} \mathbb{C}  \simeq \mathscr{H}
            is given by the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+operators"} projection operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_w
            as
          #eq:MeasurementChannelInIdeaSection.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi meas
                    %mi W
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ρ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi w
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi w
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  meas_W
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\rho
                  \\;\mapsto\;
                  \\underset{w}{\sum}
                  P_w \cdot \rho \cdot P_w
                  \\,.
          %p
            Remarkably (from the discussion at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+decoherence"} quantum decoherence
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:MeasurementChannelInIdeaSection"} (2)
            may equivalently be understood as the result of a unitary evolution
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:UnitaryChannelInIdeaSection"} (1)
            of the state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            coupled to an environment state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %em followed by
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+trace+quantum+channel"} partial trace
            over the environment’s Hilbert space. This observation turns out to generally lead to yet another characterization of quantum channels:
          %p
            Quantum channels equivalently act on a density matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            by
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                tensoring it to another state
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              %p (coupling the system to an environment/“bath”)
            %li
              %p
                sending the tensor state through a unitary channel
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:UnitaryChannelInIdeaSection"} (1)
              %p
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger evolution
                of the couplesystem)
            %li
              %p
                applying the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+trace+quantum+channel"} partial trace
                over the Hilbert space of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              %p (averaging the outcome over all states of the environment/bath).
          %p
            In this perspective, quantum channels are understood as a kind of unitary quantum gates after all, but acting on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+quantum+systems"} open quantum systems
            including their environment with the stochasticity induced (only) by (deliberate) ignorance of the environment’s state.
          %p This is discussed below at:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#QuantumChannelsAndDecoherence"} Quantum channels and Decoherence
          %p
            In this last form, the formulation of quantum channels lends itself to formulation in the
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
          %p This is discussed below at :
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#InTermsOfCompactClosedCategories"} In terms of dagger-compact closed categories
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %h3#InTermsOfPositivityConditions In terms of positivity conditions
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}_i
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+space"} finite-dimensional Hilbert space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msubsup
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Mat(\mathscr{H}_i)
                  \\,\simeq\,
                  \\mathscr{H}_i
                  \\otimes
                  \\mathscr{H}_i^\ast
          %p
            the corresponding space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
            — including as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrices"} density matrices
            representing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+states"} mixed states
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            described by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
          %p
          #PositiveMaps.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msubsup
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 1
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msubsup
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 2
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Phi
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathscr{H}_1
                    \\otimes
                    \\mathscr{H}_1^\ast
                    \\longrightarrow
                    \\mathscr{H}_2
                    \\otimes
                    \\mathscr{H}_2^\ast
            %p is called
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %em hermitian
                  iff it preserves
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+matrices"} Hermitian matrices
              %li
                %p
                  %em positive
                  iff it preserves
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+operator"} positive matrices
              %li
                %p
                  %em
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                    \-positive
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Φ
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mi Id
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi ℂ
                              %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi \otimes Id_{\mathbb{C}^n}
                  is positive for
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %li
                %p
                  %em completely positive
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Φ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
                  is
                  = succeed "-positive" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %p
              A positive
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
              is furthermore called:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+map"} stochastic
                  if it preserves the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
                  of matrices
              %li
                %p
                  %em doubly stochastic
                  if it preserves also the identity matrix.
            %p
              Finally, a completely positive
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
              is called:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %em quantum channel
                  if it is stochastic,
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+quantum+channel"} unital quantum channel
                  if it is doubly stochastic.
            %p
          %p
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Arveson69"}> Arveson 1969 §1
            ,
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#LandauStreater93"} Landau &amp; Streater 1993 p. 107-108
          %h3#InTermsOfKrausDecompositions In terms of operator-sum decompositions
          %p
          #OperatorSumDecompositionOfQuantumChannels.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (operator-sum decomposition of quantum channels)
              %br/
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}_i
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+spaces"} finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi chan
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msubsup
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 1
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msubsup
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 2
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    chan
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathscr{H}_1 \otimes \mathscr{H}_1^\ast
                    \\longrightarrow
                    \\mathscr{H}_2 \otimes \mathscr{H}_2^\ast
            %p
              is completely positive (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PositiveMaps"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+set"} indexed set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi r
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi R
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi r
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    R \,\colon\, FinSet
                    ,\;\;
                    r \,\colon\, R
                    \\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\vdash
                    \\;\;\;\;\;
                    E_r \,\colon\, \mathscr{H}_1 \longrightarrow \mathscr{H}_2
            %p
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi chan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} chan
              is the sum of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation+actions"} conjugation actions
              with these operators (“Kraus form”):
            #eq:OperatorSumDecompositionFormula.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi chan
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi r
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi r
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msubsup
                      %mi E
                      %mi r
                      %mo †
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    chan(\rho)
                    \\;=\;
                    \\underset{r}{\sum}
                    \\,
                    E_r \cdot \rho \cdot E_r^\dagger.
            %p
          Accordingly, in addition:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi chan
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} chan
                preserves the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
                and is hence a quantum channel iff
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi r
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msubsup
                        %mi E
                        %mi r
                        %mo †
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi r
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi.mathscript ℋ
                            %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\sum_r \, E_r^\dagger \cdot E_r \,=\, id_{\mathscr{H}_1}
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi chan
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} chan
                preserves also the identity matrix and is hence a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+quantum+channel"} unital quantum channel
                iff (in addition)
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi r
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi r
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msubsup
                        %mi E
                        %mi r
                        %mo †
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi.mathscript ℋ
                            %mn 2
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\sum_r \, E_r \cdot E_r^\dagger \,=\, id_{\mathscr{H}_2}
                      \\,.
          %p
            The idea goes back to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Stinespring55"} Stinespring 1955
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:OperatorSumDecompositionFormula"} (3)
            is also called
            %em Kraus decomposition
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %em Kraus operators
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Kraus71"} Kraus 1971
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OperatorSumDecompositionOfQuantumChannels"}
            is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Choi75"} Choi 1975 Thm. 1
          %p
            Review includes:
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#NielsenChuang00"} Nielsen &amp; Chuang 2000 Thm. 8.1
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Kuperberg05"} Kuperberg 2005 Thm. 1.5.1
          %p
            A general abstract proof in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+category"} †-categories
            is claimed by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Selinger05"} Selinger 2005
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Coecke07"} Coecke 2007
          %p
          #CanonicalKrausDecomposition.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (canonical operator sum-decomposition)
              %br/
              The operator-sum decomosition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi r
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_r)_{r \colon R}
              in Thm.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OperatorSumDecompositionOfQuantumChannels"}
              may always be chosen such as to be
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonality"} orthogonal
              under the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ,
                    %mi r
                    %mo ′
