              %p This concludes the construction of an environmental representation where the environment is in a pure state.
            %p
            .num_remark
              %h6 Remark
              %p The above theorem is often phrased as “… and the environment can be assumed to be in a pure state”. But in fact the proof crucially uses the assumption that the environment is in a pure state. It is not clear that there is a proof that works more generally.
              %p
                In fact, if the environment is taken to be in the maximally mixed state, then the resulting quantum channels are called
                %em noisy operations
                or
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unistochastic+quantum+channel"} unistochastic quantum channels
                and are not expected to exhaust all quantum channels.
          %h3#InTermsOfCompactClosedCategories
            In terms of
